<div class="pf-page">
  <div class="pf-wrap">
    <span class="pf-kicker">Waves &#038; Light</span>
    <h1>Speed of Light: Exact Value, Formula &#038; Why It&#8217;s a Universal Limit</h1>
    <div class="pf-quick">The speed of light in a vacuum is <strong>299,792,458 metres per second</strong> (exactly, by definition) &mdash; roughly <strong>186,282 miles per second</strong> or <strong>1.079 billion km/h</strong>. It&#8217;s usually rounded to 3 &times; 10⁸ m/s for calculations.</div>
    
<h2>What is the speed of light?</h2>
<p>The speed of light, denoted by the letter <em>c</em>, is the speed at which electromagnetic
radiation &mdash; including visible light, radio waves, X-rays, and gamma rays &mdash; travels
through a vacuum. It&#8217;s one of the most fundamental constants in physics: it doesn&#8217;t change with
your motion, the light source&#8217;s motion, or which direction you measure it in.</p>
<p>Since 1983, the metre itself has been defined in terms of <em>c</em>, which is why the value
below is exact rather than measured.</p>


<h2>The exact value, in every common unit</h2>
<table class="pf-table">
<tr><th>Unit</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Metres per second (m/s)</td><td>299,792,458</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kilometres per second (km/s)</td><td>≈ 299,792</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kilometres per hour (km/h)</td><td>≈ 1,079,252,848</td></tr>
<tr><td>Miles per second (mi/s)</td><td>≈ 186,282</td></tr>
<tr><td>Miles per hour (mph)</td><td>≈ 670,616,629</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For most physics problems, you can round <em>c</em> to <strong>3 &times; 10⁸ m/s</strong>
without meaningfully affecting your answer.</p>


<h2>Why is the speed of light a universal constant?</h2>
<p>Two results from physics pin this down:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Maxwell&#8217;s equations (1860s):</strong> James Clerk Maxwell showed that electric and
magnetic fields propagate as waves at a speed determined only by two constants of the vacuum
itself &mdash; not by the source. That speed matched the measured speed of light almost exactly,
which is how physicists first realised light <em>is</em> an electromagnetic wave.</li>
<li><strong>Special relativity (1905):</strong> Einstein showed that <em>c</em> is the same for
every observer, no matter how fast they&#8217;re moving relative to the light source. It also turns
out to be the maximum speed at which any information, matter, or energy can travel through
space.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is why you&#8217;ll sometimes see <em>c</em> called &#8220;the speed limit of the universe&#8221; rather
than just &#8220;the speed of light&#8221; &mdash; it governs causality itself, not only optics.</p>


<h2>The formula: c = λf</h2>
<div class="pf-formula"><span class="pf-flabel">Wave speed equation</span>\( c = \lambda f \)</div>
<table class="pf-table">
<tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Quantity</th><th>Unit</th></tr>
<tr><td>c</td><td>Speed of light</td><td>m/s</td></tr>
<tr><td>λ (lambda)</td><td>Wavelength</td><td>metres (m)</td></tr>
<tr><td>f</td><td>Frequency</td><td>hertz (Hz)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>This is the same wave-speed relationship used for every wave (see our
<a href="/wave-speed-frequency-wavelength/">wave speed, frequency and wavelength</a> guide) &mdash;
for light specifically, the speed term is always <em>c</em>. It tells you that wavelength and
frequency are inversely related: double the frequency, and the wavelength halves, since their
product must always equal the same constant, <em>c</em>.</p>


<h2>How was the speed of light measured?</h2>
<p>Long before lasers existed, scientists found clever ways to pin down <em>c</em>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ole Rømer (1676):</strong> Timed the eclipses of Jupiter&#8217;s moon Io from Earth and
noticed the timing shifted depending on how far Earth was from Jupiter in its orbit &mdash; the
first evidence that light takes time to travel, rather than being instantaneous.</li>
<li><strong>Hippolyte Fizeau (1849):</strong> Shone a light beam through a spinning toothed wheel
towards a distant mirror, adjusting the wheel&#8217;s speed until the returning beam was blocked by the
next tooth, letting him calculate <em>c</em> from the wheel&#8217;s rotation speed and the distance
travelled.</li>
<li><strong>Modern methods:</strong> Today, <em>c</em> is fixed by definition (not measured),
because the metre is defined using it. Extremely precise laser interferometry is instead used to
define and check the metre itself.</li>
</ul>


<h2>Does light ever slow down?</h2>
<p>Yes &mdash; <em>c</em> specifically refers to light&#8217;s speed <strong>in a vacuum</strong>. When
light passes through a transparent medium like water, glass, or air, it interacts with the
material&#8217;s atoms and travels slower. How much slower depends on the medium&#8217;s
<strong>refractive index</strong> (<em>n</em>):</p>
<div class="pf-formula"><span class="pf-flabel">Speed of light in a medium</span>\( v = \dfrac{c}{n} \)</div>
<p>For example, light travels at roughly 0.75<em>c</em> in water (n ≈ 1.33) and around
0.66<em>c</em> in ordinary glass (n ≈ 1.5). This slowing &mdash; and the fact that it varies
slightly by wavelength &mdash; is exactly what causes refraction and the rainbow spread you see
through a prism.</p>


<h2>Worked example: light travel time from the Sun to Earth</h2>
<div class="pf-example">
<h4>How long does sunlight take to reach Earth?</h4>
<ol>
<li>Average Sun&ndash;Earth distance: <em>d</em> = 1.496 &times; 10¹¹ m</li>
<li>Speed of light: <em>c</em> = 3 &times; 10⁸ m/s</li>
<li>Time = distance ÷ speed = (1.496 &times; 10¹¹) ÷ (3 &times; 10⁸)</li>
<li>Time ≈ 499 seconds ≈ <strong>8 minutes 19 seconds</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>That&#8217;s why, if the Sun suddenly vanished, we&#8217;d still see it shining for a little over 8 more
minutes.</p>
</div>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="pf-faq">
<details><summary>Why can&#8217;t anything travel faster than light?</summary><p>Special relativity shows that as an object with mass approaches the speed of light, the energy needed to keep accelerating it grows without limit — reaching or exceeding <em>c</em> would require infinite energy. Massless particles like photons are the only things that travel at exactly <em>c</em>.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the speed of light the same in every direction?</summary><p>Yes. Every experiment ever performed shows <em>c</em> is identical in all directions and for all observers, regardless of their own motion — this is one of the two founding postulates of special relativity.</p></details>
<details><summary>What is the speed of light in a vacuum vs. in air?</summary><p>In a vacuum, light travels at exactly 299,792,458 m/s. In air, it&#8217;s only marginally slower (about 0.03% less, since air&#8217;s refractive index is very close to 1), which is why the two values are often used interchangeably in everyday calculations.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why is the speed of light exactly 299,792,458 m/s and not a rounder number?</summary><p>Because the metre is defined from the speed of light, not the other way around. In 1983, the metre was redefined as the distance light travels in 1/299,792,458 of a second, which fixed <em>c</em> at that exact value rather than something rounder.</p></details>
</div>
    <div class="pf-related"><h2>Related physics topics</h2><ul>
<li><a href="/wave-speed-frequency-wavelength/">Wave speed, frequency &#038; wavelength</a></li>
<li><a href="/electromagnetic-waves/">Electromagnetic waves</a></li>
<li><a href="/transverse-waves/">Transverse waves</a></li>
<li><a href="/kinematic-equations/">Kinematic equations</a></li>
</ul></div>
  </div>
</div>
